      "writtenOn": "2024-12-11T14:14:59Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "`Just to clarify: is the lock only on the pointers themselves, or also on the data in the buffers?` - both: when the call is using the `FFA_MAP_RXTX` we end up setting the pointers which keep the hypervisor VAs in the rx/tx.\n\nTX - the channel from the host/guest -\u003e hypervisor -\u003e TZ\nRX - the channel from TZ -\u003e hypervisor -\u003e host/guest\n\nDuring the memory sharing calls all the transactions go through the hypervisors buffers (which is only one RX/TX pair). In other words for (TX - eg MEM_SHARE call) we have to copy the data from the guest/host buffers into the hypervisor buffers (which are mapped in EL3).\nOn the RX part is a bit tricky because we only copy the data coming from EL3 on behalf of a call (eg. memory reclaim).\n\nI don\u0027t know how the buffer ownership will solve this TBH",
      "parentUuid": "e45e24f7_a9fdb5ee",
      "revId": "b5de1d48728c1078fbbd58b73e7475f67781ade7",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "2207d1b4_265444d0",
        "filename": "arch/arm64/kvm/hyp/nvhe/ffa.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 58,
      "author": {
        "id": 1809582
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-12-13T06:00:51Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Right, the hypervisor buffers are only shared with the SPMC. According to the spec, the TX buffer is for sending data from the hypervisor to the SPMC, and the RX buffer is for the opposite direction.\n\nYeah I think we don\u0027t need a lock for the RX buffer, as long as the hypervisor calls FFA_RX_RELEASE when it\u0027s done with whatever data it got in the buffer.",
      "parentUuid": "8d55f74f_8509498a",
      "revId": "b5de1d48728c1078fbbd58b73e7475f67781ade7",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
